==Portuguese==
[[Image:Alopekis puppies.jpg|thumb|250px|cachorros (sense 1)]]
[[Image:Bernese Dog 10m..jpg|thumb|250px|cachorro (sense 2)]]

===Etymology===
Of uncertain origin<ref>https://www.infopedia.pt/dicionarios/lingua-portuguesa/cachorro</ref>, perhaps from {{der|pt|eu|txakur}}, with metathesis, or from a {{der|pt|VL.|*cattulus}}<ref>https://houaiss.uol.com.br/corporativo/apps/uol_www/v6-0/html/index.php#1</ref>, from {{der|pt|la|catulus}}, and possibly via {{der|pt|es|cachorro||cub}}. Compare also {{cog|it|cucciolo}} and {{cog|co|ghjacaru}}.

===Pronunciation===
{{pt-IPA|cachôrro}}
* {{a|South Brazil}} {{IPA|pt|/kaˈʃo.χo/}}
* {{a|Rural Central Brazil}} {{IPA|pt|/kɐˈʃo.hʷ/}}
* {{hyphenation|pt|ca|chor|ro}}

===Noun===
{{pt-noun|m|f=+}}

# {{lb|pt|Portugal}} [[puppy]] {{gloss|a young dog}}
# {{lb|pt|Brazil|Madeira|colloquial}} [[dog]] (of any age)
# {{lb|pt|Brazil|derogatory}} a [[promiscuous]] man
# {{lb|pt|by extension}} an unfaithful man
# {{short for|pt|cachorro-quente|dot=:}} [[hot dog]]

====Usage notes====
In Brazil, this is the neutral and colloquial term for "dog", whereas in settings of higher formalities {{m|pt|cão}} is used instead. The feminine {{m|pt|cachorra}} is even more avoided in such settings (in which {{m|pt|cadela|cadela}} is used), but is used more often in everyday language.

====Derived terms====
* {{l|pt|cachorrão|pos=augmentative}}
* {{l|pt|cachorrinho|pos=diminutive}}
* {{l|pt|cachorro-do-mato}}
* {{l|pt|cachorro-quente|pos=hot-dog}}

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|dog}} {{l|pt|cão}}

